## Topic: Wetlands

Wetlands are areas where water covers or saturates the soil for part or all of the year. Although wetlands were once considered unproductive areas, scientists now recognize that they provide several important ecological functions.

One important function of wetlands is water purification. Wetland plants and soil can trap certain pollutants before they reach rivers and lakes. Wetlands can also absorb excess water during periods of heavy rainfall, helping mitigate the effects of flooding.

In addition, wetlands provide habitats for many species of plants and animals. However, human development has caused the loss of many wetlands around the world. Protecting these ecosystems is therefore important for sustaining biodiversity and reducing some environmental risks.

